1
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:28,500
She's from the leash and license set... he's footloose and collar free!

2
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Lady and the Tramp is a family, animation, romance
film directed by Hamilton Luske.

3
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:13,500
Lady, a golden cocker spaniel, meets up with a
mongrel dog who calls himself the Tramp.

4
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:36,000
He is obviously from the wrong side of town, but happenings at Lady's
home make her decide to travel with him for a while.

5
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:58,500
Lady (voice), played by Barbara Luddy.

6
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:21,000
Tramp (voice), played by Larry Roberts.

7
00:16:37,500 --> 00:16:43,500
Darling / Si / Am / Peg (voice), played by Peggy Lee.

8
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:06,000
Jock / Bull / Policeman at Zoo / Dachsie / Joe (voice), played by Bill Thompson.

9
00:21:22,500 --> 00:21:28,500
Trusty (voice), played by Bill Baucom.

10
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:51,000
Beaver (voice), played by Stan Freberg.

11
00:26:07,500 --> 00:26:13,500
Lady and the Tramp is a 1955 American animated musical romantic comedy film produced by
Walt Disney Productions and released by Buena Vista Film Distribution.

12
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:36,000
Based on Ward Greene's 1945 Cosmopolitan magazine story "Happy Dan, the Cynical Dog",
it was directed by Hamilton Luske, Clyde Geronimi, and Wilfred Jackson.

13
00:30:52,500 --> 00:30:58,500
The film features the voices of Peggy Lee, Barbara Luddy, Larry Roberts, Bill Thompson, Bill Baucom,
Stan Freberg, Verna Felton, Alan Reed, George Givot, Dallas McKennon, and Lee Millar.

14
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:21,000
The film follows Lady, the pampered Cocker Spaniel, as she grows from puppy to adult, deals with
changes in her family, and meets and falls in love with the homeless mutt Tramp.

15
00:35:37,500 --> 00:35:43,500
The inspiration for Lady and the Tramp originated in 1925 when Walt Disney
presented his wife Lillian with a chow puppy in a hatbox.

16
00:38:00,000 --> 00:38:06,000
In 1937, Joe Grant, a storyboard artist, pitched to Disney an
original idea as inspired by his English Springer Spaniel.

17
00:40:22,500 --> 00:40:28,500
Throughout the 1940s, the project underwent numerous story revisions, but it
was shelved due to Disney's production of several package films.

18
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:51,000
Grant left the Disney studios in 1949.

19
00:45:07,500 --> 00:45:13,500
In 1952, Disney placed the project back into active development, and the
final story was revised by Erdman Penner and Joe Rinaldi.

20
00:47:30,000 --> 00:47:36,000
The film's art direction was supervised by Claude Coats, who
took inspiration from gingerbread architecture.

21
00:49:52,500 --> 00:49:58,500
Peggy Lee and Sonny Burke wrote the film's original songs, while
Oliver Wallace composed the instrumental musical score.

22
00:52:15,000 --> 00:52:21,000
During production, Disney decided Lady and the Tramp would be the first animated
film to be filmed in the CinemaScope widescreen film process.

23
00:54:37,500 --> 00:54:43,500
It was also Disney's first animated film to be distributed by their Buena
Vista division following their split from RKO Radio Pictures.

24
00:57:00,000 --> 00:57:06,000
Lady and the Tramp was released in theaters on June 22,
1955, and achieved box office success.

25
00:59:22,500 --> 00:59:28,500
While it initially received mixed reviews from critics, the film's critical reception has grown more
favorable over time, and it is now regarded as one of the greatest animated films of all time.

26
01:01:45,000 --> 01:01:51,000
The film launched a long-running Disney comic strip featuring Scamp.

27
01:04:07,500 --> 01:04:13,500
A direct-to-video sequel, titled Lady and the Tramp II:
Scamp's Adventure, was released in 2001.

28
01:06:30,000 --> 01:06:36,000
A live-action/CGI hybrid remake premiered in 2019 as a
launch title for the Disney+ streaming service.

29
01:08:52,500 --> 01:08:58,500
In 2023, Lady and the Tramp was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by
the Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically or aesthetically significant."

30
01:11:15,000 --> 01:11:21,000
The film explores themes of love of one's life,
spaghetti, female lover, cartoon, villain.

31
01:13:37,500 --> 01:13:43,500
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
